{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Chopra1986-ZnS-MgF2-3stack",
  "Source": "K. N. Chopra and K. V. Narasimham, \"Thin film coating design and fabrication of broadband dielectric coated laser mirrors,\" J. Optics (Paris) 17, 107-111 (1986). doi:10.1088/0150-536X/17/2/006 - Fig. 2 caption and text p. 110-111 (Sec. 2); general design formula p. 108 (Sec. 1); indices from Table 1 row 6 (ZnS 2.35, MgF2 1.38)",
  "Notes": "Broadband visible laser mirror with high reflectance at 0.52, 0.63 and 0.74 um (blue-green, He-Ne and far-red dye lasers): three detuned five-layer ZnS/MgF2 quarter-wave stacks\nSpecification: High reflectance (each 5-layer stack ~85 % expected) at 0.52, 0.63 and 0.74 um and >= 85 % across 0.49-0.81 um; stacks in 'perfect contact' (stop bands touching) via tuning ratios T2/T1 = 1.176 and T1/T3 = 1.214 for ZnS/MgF2.\nReported: Text p. 111: each stack of 5 layers gives ~85 % reflectivity at each of the three wavelengths; the coated sample gives about 93 % R at 0.52 um, about 94 % at 0.63 um and about 92 % at 0.74 um, and 85 % or more over 0.49-0.81 um (assuming negligible absorption/scattering, R = 1 - T from the measurement). Fig. 2 (measured transmission, Cary 17D, read off the plot, approximate): T falls from ~0.4 near 490 nm to a broad minimum of ~0.03-0.05 over ~520-760 nm with a small bump of ~0.07 around 590-600 nm, then rises to ~0.55 at 880 nm; a sharp T peak of ~0.4 near 487 nm on the short-wave edge. Table 1 row 6 (ZnS 2.35 / MgF2 1.38): nH/nL = 1.703, epsilon = 0.2544 rad (stop-band half-width for the basic tuning, Eqs. 1-2), T2/T1 = (pi+eps)/(pi-eps) = 1.176, T1/T3 = (pi-eps)/(pi-3 eps) = 1.214 (Eqs. 4-5).\nNotes: Paper's design formula (p. 108): Glass | (T+dT)(HL)^n3 T(HL)^n2 (T-dT)(HL)^n1 | Air, written from the glass side; here it is reversed so layer 1 is next to air. The fabricated mirror has each stack as HLHLH (Fig. 2 caption), so the stack-to-stack junctions are two adjacent H layers of different tuning; they are kept as separate layers. Text: T1 = 0.6328 um and T2 = 0.7442 um, T3 = 0.5213 um; per the formula the T+dT (744.2 nm) stack is on the glass and the T-dT (521.3 nm) stack faces air. Thicknesses are quarter waves at 521.3, 632.8 and 744.2 nm, written as QWOT at 632.8 nm (521.3/632.8 = 0.823799, 744.2/632.8 = 1.176043). 15 layers total (paper: three stacks of 5 layers; no total thickness printed). Glass substrate index is not given (only 'glass'; n_g appears symbolically in Eq. 6); n = 1.52 is an assumption. Normal incidence assumed (not stated). Indices are the non-dispersive values from Table 1; the measured coating will differ, so the reported R values are measurements, not design calculations. Incident side assumed to be the coated face in air; back-surface reflection not discussed.\nLayers from Fig. 2 caption and text p. 110-111 (Sec. 2); general design formula p. 108 (Sec. 1); indices from Table 1 row 6 (ZnS 2.35, MgF2 1.38); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours R 93 / 97 / 95 % at 520 / 630 / 740 nm; >= 85 % from 480 to 820 nm (min 4.02 % over 490-810); paper measured ~93 / 94 / 92 %; >= 85 % from 490 to 810 nm. Written as the paper writes it, the three HLHLH stacks meet H to H; each junction is then a half-wave ZnS spacer, and the calculation passes two narrow bands, near 573 nm (R 4 %) and 695 nm, which the measured curve does not show (only a small bump near 595 nm). The deposited mirror probably differed from the formula; the 520, 630 and 740 nm values agree within 3 points.",
